  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Let the pyramid have base side a and height H, and write the cylinder height as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A horizontal section at height h is a square of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: a vertical section through the apex shows this linear scale by similar triangles.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The upper circular face must fit in that square, so its radius is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. A centred circle attains this bound, and every lower pyramid section is larger, so the entire vertical cylinder fits. Thus the largest cylinder for a given u has volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>u</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The pyramid volume is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, giving the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. The dimensions cancel, so the stated equality of pyramid edge lengths need not be used further.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> Differentiate the polynomial factor: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. It is positive for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and negative for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; the degenerate endpoint volumes are zero. Hence the attained maximum occurs at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>5.</strong> The ratio is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, answer A. The centred cylinder described above proves that the bound is achievable.</p>"
